---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const PERM = "server/src/iki_server/org/permissions.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const MODELS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/models.py";
const SERVICES = "server/src/iki_server/entities/services.py";
const REQ = "server/src/iki_server/entities/requests.py";

const H = {
  short: "A record is read through the projects it's linked into",
  how: "How a read is scoped, and how a record reaches another project",
  rules: "What may be shared, and what stays",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "requests", label: "What each project in a request can read" },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Every record has an owner project and a <code>ProjectLink</code> into it. Reading a record takes
      a role in any project it's linked into; changing it takes Editor in its owner project. Sharing a
      record with another project adds a link, never a copy, and only when its owner project allows
      sharing and both are in the same registry (<Adr n={[35, 67, 115]} />). A request between
      projects links what it sends into the service, and its released results back, marking each link{" "}
      <code>via_request</code>: what a request linked is read there, and never passed on (<Adr
        n={[132, 149]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Visible projects.</strong> Those with a membership for the caller, or every project
        for an Org Admin; none without the <code>read</code> scope; and only the token's projects (<Path
          p={PERM}
          sym="def visible_projects("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Readable projects.</strong> Of those, the ones where the caller has a role. That's why
        an Org Admin sees a project but not its data (<Path p={ACCESS} sym="def readable_projects(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Readable records.</strong> Those with a link into a readable project. A view is
        readable only if every record it shows is too, and a purged record's tombstone stays readable
        to those who could read it (<Adr n={[92, 110]} />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def visible_of" />, <Path
          p={ACCESS}
          sym="def tombstones("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Readable files.</strong> Those added to a readable project, or used by a record the
        caller reads, at any revision (<Path p={ACCESS} sym="def readable_files(" />). Files themselves
        are on <PageLink to="How data links to records" />.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>A link is a row per record and project, unique for the pair:</p>
    <Excerpt file={MODELS} start="^class ProjectLink\(" />
    <p>
      An Editor in a project links a record they read into it with <code
        >PUT /entities/{"{ref}"}/projects/{"{project_id}"}</code
      >. The link is audited as <code>entity.linked</code>, since it changes who can read the record.
      Unlinking takes it out again, except from its owner project, and except what a request still
      needs (<Path p={SERVICES} sym="def link(" />, <Path p={SERVICES} sym="def unlink(" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="requests">What each project in a request can read</h3>
    <p>
      A request is owned by the project that asks. Sending it links the request and the records its{" "}
      <code>sends</code> fields name into the service's project; records named later, when the asker
      edits a sent request, go too. Releasing a result links it back into the asking project. Each of
      those links names the request in <code>via_request</code> (<Path p={REQ} sym="def _link(" />).
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>The asking project</th><th>The service</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>The request</td><td>Owns it</td><td>Reads it; it stays as the service's record of what it was asked</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Records it sends</td><td>Owns them</td><td>Reads them; the link stays while the request names them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A result, before release</td><td>Can't read it</td><td>Owns it; nobody can share it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A result, once released</td><td>Reads it, for good</td><td>Owns it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A work item</td><td>Can't read it</td><td>Owns it; it's usable only there</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Neither side can share onward what it reads through a request: <code>shareable_into</code> counts
      only links the person reads a record through their own way, those with no <code
        >via_request</code
      >. A service that doesn't allow sharing can't release. How requests are asked, served and
      completed is on <PageLink to="A request's life" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Every kind of sharing goes through one check" open>
        <p>
          Linking, references from another project's records, adding a run's inputs to its project,
          reuse, and sending with a request all ask <code>shareable_into</code>: the records the person reads that this project may use.
          They're its own, those already in it, and those whose project allows sharing and is in its
          registry, which the person reads other than through a request, and which aren't results
          awaiting release. A work item is usable only in its own project (<Adr n={[67, 115, 132]} />):
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={ACCESS} start="^def shareable_into\(" />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Why a record is held back">
        <p>
          When a project may not use a record the person reads, <code>why_held</code> says which of
          five reasons it is, and the refusal or the reference's error says it in words (<Path
            p={SERVICES}
            sym="def unshareable("
          />):
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={ACCESS} start="^def why_held\(" />
        <p>
          Files have their own, <code>why_held_file</code>: another registry, read through a request,
          or a project that doesn't allow sharing. A file that came with a request is read with the
          records it came on, and nothing else refers to it anew (<Path p={ACCESS} sym="def shareable_files(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What a request's links keep">
        <p>
          A link a request made can't be taken out while the request needs it: the request in its
          service, a record it still sends, and a result released back. A release is told once: a
          result already linked back for this request isn't linked again, and one already in the
          asking project some other way is marked released where it is (<Path
            p={REQ}
            sym="def why_kept("
          />, <Path p={REQ} sym="Released already: a release is told once." />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Registries share nothing">
        <p>
          Projects in different registries share no records. A registry entry belongs to no project:
          it's readable by whoever can read a record of it, so its ID alone grants nothing (<Adr
            n="0115"
          />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def visible_entries(" />). Registries are on <PageLink
            to="Identity and HRIDs"
          />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Runs read only what's in their project">
        <p>
          A run's inputs must be linked into its project. A launcher who reads one from elsewhere is
          asked to add it, which is a link like any other; MCP agents aren't offered it, because
          widening who can read data is the person's decision (<Adr n="0066" />). What a run reads of
          the references its inputs name is on <PageLink to="Unreadable references" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Tested with another project's objects">
        <p>
          The access matrix lists every operation, and the IDOR tests replay each with another
          project's objects. Requests are fuzzed both ways: of every record either project holds, each
          side reads, lists and searches exactly what the table above says (<Adr n={[101, 132]} />, <Path
            p="server/tests/test_api_access.py"
          />, <Path p="server/tests/test_api_idor.py" />, <Path p="server/tests/test_worker_idor.py" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Routes</h3>
      <Routes paths={/^\/api\/v1\/entities\/\{ref\}\/projects/} />
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={ACCESS} />: what a caller can read and share</li>
        <li><Path p={MODELS} sym="class ProjectLink(" />: the links</li>
        <li><Path p={REQ} />: the links a request makes</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0035" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0067" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0115" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0132" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0149" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
